From 5a8d300ddff6e1240e59a45960356792d4019fcb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Luzar <5153846+dwelle@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 11:52:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] perf(editor): stop inverting the interactive canvas via
 CSS filter in dark mode (#12050)

The interactive canvas (selection outlines, transform handles, binding
highlights, collaborator cursors, snap lines, scrollbars) was still
composited through `filter: invert(93%) hue-rotate(180deg)` in dark mode
(#10578 only de-filtered the static canvas). The canvas covers the whole
viewport and repaints on nearly every pointer move, so browsers that
composite in software (Firefox/Zen on software WebRender or with hardware
acceleration disabled) paid for a full-viewport color-matrix pass per frame
and dropped from 120fps to ~40fps during marquee/drag. Light mode was
unaffected.

Colors are now mapped in JS via `applyDarkModeFilter`, like the static
canvas. Hardcoded dark-mode values are the exact post-filter equivalents of
the previous ones, so rendering is unchanged, with two intentional
exceptions:

- collaborator cursors and remote selection outlines render in the
  collaborators' actual colors (matching their avatars) instead of inverted
  ones (per the existing TODO in clients.ts)
- the lasso trail now uses the theme's `--color-selection` like the marquee
  selection, instead of a hardcoded light-theme purple that never matched in
  dark mode

`--color-selection` for the dark theme now holds the actual rendered color
(#b4b0ff); hosts overriding it no longer need to pre-invert.

Co-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit f1a79b73df5b9883f940417861a4f5fb879a3f02)
